<%#
  One page, four decisions: the domain passkeys bind to, where auth mail comes
  from, which ways in are open, and the two HMAC keys.

  Everything on it is a VALUE. The parts of SparrowAuth::Configuration that are
  code -- the provisioning callbacks, the roles a host declares -- are not
  here and cannot be, and the list at the bottom says where they live instead.

  Nothing here is hidden by the server. The script at the end only tells you,
  while you type, that you are about to change the one setting that cannot be
  changed later. Turn scripting off and the page is complete, every control is
  reachable, and the same warning is already written underneath the box.

  These are console pages and Tailwind's `@source` scans this directory by
  name. Views for the console belong here and nowhere else: sparrow_auth's
  end-user sign-in pages are hand-styled with `sa-*` names and no Tailwind, and
  a console page filed among them compiles to nothing at all.
%>
<h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Authentication</h1>
<p class="mt-1 text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  Passkeys are the primary way in and are always on &mdash; they are the only method that cannot be
  phished. Everything below either supports them or offers a way in beside them.
</p>
<p class="mt-2 text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  This page is about getting people <em>in</em>. What they are allowed to do once they are in is
  decided by your own code. A role is a name SparrowKit stores and never interprets, so compare it
  yourself &mdash;
  <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1 py-0.5 text-sm dark:bg-slate-800">current_account.role_in(current_organization)</code>
  &mdash; in your controllers. There is nothing to set here.
</p>
<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  Everything on this page is written to
  <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">sparrow_auth:</code>
  in your application's Rails encrypted credentials.
</p>

<% unless @writable %>
  <div role="alert"
       class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-900 dark:bg-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
    <p class="font-medium">This page cannot save anything yet</p>
    <p class="mt-1"><%= @not_writable_reason %></p>
  </div>
<% end %>

<form id="auth_form" action="<%= sparrowkit_path %>" method="post" autocomplete="off" class="mt-8">
  <%#
    Written out rather than built by form_with. Tailwind only compiles classes
    it can find in a file, and a class inside an ERB tag is invisible both to
    the build's scanner and to the spec that checks the build is not stale.
  %>
  <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="patch" autocomplete="off">
  <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>" autocomplete="off">

  <section class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Passkeys</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      The primary way in, and the only one that cannot be phished. It is always on.
    </p>

    <%#
      Shown while nothing is stored, which is every application on the day it
      installs this.

      The hint under the box explains what the setting is; this says why it
      cannot wait. The existing warning below fires only when CHANGING a stored
      value, so a developer who never set one at all met no urgency anywhere --
      and the installer used to say this in the terminal, where it scrolled past
      once and was gone.
    %>
    <%#
      Fires on the EFFECTIVE domain rather than on this gem's stored one. The
      urgency is "passkeys have no domain to bind to", and since the domain is
      normally inherited, an application that has answered on the front page is
      not in that state even though it has stored nothing here.
    %>
    <% if @effective_rp_id.blank? %>
      <div role="status"
           class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-900 dark:bg-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
        <p class="font-medium">Set your application's address before you deploy</p>
        <p class="mt-1">
          Its domain is what passkeys are permanently bound to, and it is the one setting that a
          later change cannot undo. A passkey cannot be moved to a different domain, so getting it
          wrong is not fixed by editing anything — it is fixed by every one of your users enrolling
          again.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1">
          With no address set, the domain is whichever origin the request happens to arrive on.
          That is right on your own machine and a coin toss in production. Set it on the
          <a href="/sparrowkit" class="font-medium underline">SparrowKit home page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <%#
        "Relying Party ID" is the WebAuthn specification's name for it, and it
        is a term of art from a document nobody building a product has read.
        The Relying Party is the site relying on the authenticator to vouch for
        somebody -- so the ID is just the site's domain.

        The plain name leads; the specification's name follows in the hint,
        because it is the word in every error message, browser console warning
        and search result a developer will meet later, and a page that never
        says it leaves them unable to connect the two.
      %>
      <p class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
        Your website's domain
      </p>

      <%#
        Shown before it is offered for editing, because it has already been
        answered. The address on the front page is the one place this is typed,
        and a second empty box asking for it again is how the two come to
        disagree -- with a passkey domain, disagreeing means every enrolled
        passkey stops working and cannot be migrated.

        The override below is not decoration. A site served at
        www.example.com usually wants passkeys on example.com, so that they keep
        working on the apex and on every other subdomain, and that is a
        different value from the address by design rather than by mistake.
      %>
      <div class="mt-1 rounded-md border border-slate-300 bg-slate-50 px-3 py-2 dark:border-slate-700 dark:bg-slate-800/50">
        <p class="font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
          <%= @inherited_rp_id.presence || "not set yet" %>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          <% if @inherited_rp_id.present? %>
            Taken from the address on the
            <a href="/sparrowkit" class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">SparrowKit home page</a>.
            Change it there and this follows.
          <% else %>
            Set the address on the
            <a href="/sparrowkit" class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">SparrowKit home page</a>
            and this fills in. Until then passkeys use whatever domain the browser asked from, which
            is fine on your own machine and not something to deploy.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-3 flex items-start gap-2">
        <input type="hidden" name="webauthn_rp_id_override" value="0" autocomplete="off">
        <input type="checkbox"
               id="auth_webauthn_rp_id_override"
               name="webauthn_rp_id_override"
               value="1"
               <%= "checked" if @overriding_rp_id %>
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               data-inherited-rp-id="<%= @inherited_rp_id %>"
               class="mt-0.5 size-4 rounded border-slate-500 text-purple-900 focus:ring-purple-900 dark:text-purple-300 dark:focus:ring-purple-300">
        <label for="auth_webauthn_rp_id_override" class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-200">
          Use a different domain for passkeys
          <span class="block text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
            Only if the passkey domain is not the same as your site's address.
          </span>
        </label>
      </div>

      <div id="auth_rp_id_override_field" <%= "hidden" unless @overriding_rp_id %> class="mt-2">
      <label for="auth_webauthn_rp_id" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
        The domain passkeys bind to<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if @stored_rp_id.present? %>
      </label>
      <%#
        The scheme shown as a fixed prefix rather than asked for and then
        refused.

        The hint underneath says "no https://", and a box that says nothing
        while you type one is a box that teaches by rejection: paste the
        address out of a browser bar, press Save, read an error. Printing the
        part that is NOT yours to type puts the rule where the mistake happens.

        It is decoration, not a value -- the prefix is outside the input, so
        the field still posts a bare domain and the server still checks it.
        aria-hidden for the same reason: a screen reader announcing "https://
        edit, your website's domain" would describe a control that does not
        exist. The hint carries the rule in words.

        The border moves to the wrapper and comes off the input, so the two
        read as one control. focus-within puts the focus ring on the whole
        group, which is what a sighted developer is looking at.
      %>
      <%#
        The group carries the input's surface and the prefix sits a step above
        it, so the two are told apart without a second border: the field is the
        recessed part, the prefix the fixed part.
      %>
      <div class="mt-1 flex w-full items-stretch overflow-hidden rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 focus-within:border-purple-900 focus-within:ring-1 focus-within:ring-purple-900 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:focus-within:border-purple-300 dark:focus-within:ring-purple-300">
        <span aria-hidden="true"
              class="flex select-none items-center border-r border-slate-500 bg-slate-200 px-3 font-mono text-sm text-slate-600 dark:bg-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300">https://</span>
        <input type="text"
               id="auth_webauthn_rp_id"
               name="webauthn_rp_id"
               value="<%= @rp_id %>"
               placeholder="example.com"
               autocomplete="off"
               spellcheck="false"
               data-stored-rp-id="<%= @stored_rp_id %>"
               aria-describedby="auth_webauthn_rp_id_hint"
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               class="block w-full border-0 bg-transparent px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-900 focus:outline-none disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-100 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
      </div>
      <p id="auth_webauthn_rp_id_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
        A passkey is locked to one domain. Somebody who enrols one at your site can use it at
        your site and nowhere else — that is what makes it unphishable, because a copy of your
        login page on another domain cannot ask for it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
        Write the bare domain: no
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">https://</code>,
        no port, no path. Use
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">example.com</code>
        rather than <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">www.example.com</code>,
        because the bare form also covers
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">www.</code>,
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">app.</code>
        and every other subdomain, while a passkey enrolled at
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">www.example.com</code>
        stops working the day somebody visits without the www.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-300">
        Elsewhere this is called the <strong class="font-medium">Relying Party ID</strong>, or RP ID —
        the name the WebAuthn standard uses, and the one you will see in browser errors and
        documentation.
      </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%#
      Rendered by the server in the state it should be in: visible when a
      refused save is showing a changed value, hidden otherwise. The script at
      the bottom keeps it honest while somebody types. The same sentence is in
      the hint above either way, so nothing is only in the warning.
    %>
    <div id="auth_rp_id_change"
         role="status"
         <%= "hidden" unless @rp_id_changing %>
         class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-900 dark:bg-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
      <p class="font-medium">Changing this asks every user to enrol again</p>
      <p class="mt-1">
        Passkeys already enrolled against
        <code data-stored-rp-id-label class="font-mono"><%= @stored_rp_id %></code>
        will not work against a different Relying Party ID. A credential is bound to the ID it
        was created under and cannot be moved to another, so this is not a mistake a later
        change of setting can undo. It is undone by everybody enrolling again.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-4">
      <%#
        Shown, not asked for, on the same terms as the domain above. The product
        has one name and the front page took it; a second box here is a second
        answer, and the two would drift the first time somebody renamed
        anything.
      %>
      <p class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
        Your product's name
      </p>

      <div class="mt-1 rounded-md border border-slate-300 bg-slate-50 px-3 py-2 dark:border-slate-700 dark:bg-slate-800/50">
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
          <%= @inherited_rp_name.presence || "not set yet" %>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          <% if @inherited_rp_name.present? %>
            Taken from the name on the
            <a href="/sparrowkit" class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">SparrowKit home page</a>.
            Change it there and this follows.
          <% else %>
            Set the name on the
            <a href="/sparrowkit" class="font-medium text-purple-900 hover:underline dark:text-purple-300">SparrowKit home page</a>
            and this fills in. Until then people see the domain instead, which reads like a machine
            talking.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
        Your users see this in the box their phone or laptop pops up when they sign in —
        “Sign in to <strong class="font-medium"><%= @effective_rp_name.presence || "Acme Corp" %></strong>?”.
        It should be the name they know you by, not a domain.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-3 flex items-start gap-2">
        <input type="hidden" name="webauthn_rp_name_override" value="0" autocomplete="off">
        <input type="checkbox"
               id="auth_webauthn_rp_name_override"
               name="webauthn_rp_name_override"
               value="1"
               <%= "checked" if @overriding_rp_name %>
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               class="mt-0.5 size-4 rounded border-slate-500 text-purple-900 focus:ring-purple-900 dark:text-purple-300 dark:focus:ring-purple-300">
        <label for="auth_webauthn_rp_name_override" class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-200">
          Use a different name in the passkey prompt
          <span class="block text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
            Only if it should not say what the product is called.
          </span>
        </label>
      </div>

      <div id="auth_rp_name_override_field" <%= "hidden" unless @overriding_rp_name %> class="mt-2">
        <label for="auth_webauthn_rp_name" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
          The name the passkey prompt shows<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if @stored_tree_rp_name.present? %>
        </label>
        <input type="text"
               id="auth_webauthn_rp_name"
               name="webauthn_rp_name"
               value="<%= @rp_name %>"
               placeholder="Acme Corp"
               autocomplete="off"
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-900 disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Auth mail</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      The email SparrowKit sends on your behalf when somebody signs in or joins.
      Who should it appear to come from?
    </p>

    <%#
      The short answer is above; the rest is behind a disclosure rather than a
      tooltip or a modal. It is several sentences and a list -- too much for a
      hover, which is unreachable on a touchscreen and gone the moment you look
      away -- and <details> is native: keyboard reachable, findable by the
      browser's own in-page search, and working with scripting off, which a
      modal of our own would not be without JavaScript this console does not
      otherwise need.
    %>
    <details class="mt-3 rounded-md border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-950">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer px-3 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-purple-900 dark:text-purple-300">
        What is auth mail, and when does it go out?
      </summary>
      <div class="border-t border-slate-200 px-3 py-3 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:border-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300">
        <p>
          Signing in here does not use a password by default. Instead, SparrowKit emails
          the person a short code, or a link, at the moment they ask for it. That email is
          auth mail. If it does not arrive, nobody gets in — so it is the most important
          mail your application sends.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-3">There are four kinds, and a person's own action triggers every one:</p>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li><span class="font-medium">Sign-in codes</span> — someone asked to sign in.</li>
          <li><span class="font-medium">Verification</span> — confirming a new address belongs to them.</li>
          <li><span class="font-medium">Invitations</span> — someone on your team asked to add them.</li>
          <li><span class="font-medium">Security notices</span> — telling an account owner about something that happened to it.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mt-3">
          It never goes out in bulk and nobody subscribes to it, which is why this page
          offers no choice about which sending stream carries it. Auth mail is
          transactional, always. If you set up a separate marketing provider on the Mail
          panel, this does not go through it — deliberately: a bulk sending reputation is
          the thing most likely to stop sign-in codes arriving.
        </p>

        <p class="mt-3">
          The address below is only what recipients see. Which provider actually delivers
          it is configured on the Mail panel, and leaving these blank falls back to the
          default sender set there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <label for="auth_sender_name" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Sender name<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if @stored_sender_name.present? %><%= render "sparrow_ui/console/optional" %></label>
        <input type="text"
               id="auth_sender_name"
               name="sender_name"
               value="<%= @sender_name %>"
               placeholder="Acme"
               autocomplete="off"
               aria-describedby="auth_sender_name_hint"
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-900 disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
        <p id="auth_sender_name_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          What recipients see it is from.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <label for="auth_sender_email" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Sender email<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if @stored_sender_email.present? %></label>
        <input type="email"
               id="auth_sender_email"
               name="sender_email"
               value="<%= @sender_email %>"
               placeholder="no-reply@acme.test"
               autocomplete="off"
               aria-describedby="auth_sender_email_hint"
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-900 disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
        <p id="auth_sender_email_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
          Leave both blank to use the default sender from the Mail panel.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Ways in</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Two things here are settled and have no switch, deliberately.
      <strong>Passkeys are always on</strong> &mdash; they are the primary way in, and the only
      one that cannot be phished, guessed or reused from somebody else's breach.
      <strong>Email verification is always on</strong> &mdash; an unverified address is a claim,
      not an identity, and every application that has ever added a flag for this has eventually
      shipped with it off somewhere.
    </p>

    <%#
      A radio and not two checkboxes, because the two states checkboxes can
      reach are both wrong: both on is two ways into the same account for no
      benefit, and both off is an application nobody can get into. A control
      that cannot express a mistake beats a control that refuses one.
    %>
    <fieldset class="mt-6 border-t border-slate-200 pt-5 dark:border-slate-800">
      <legend class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
        What an emailed sign-in sends
      </legend>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
        Somebody types their address; this is what arrives. One or the other &mdash; both would be
        two doors into the same account, and neither would leave a brand new account with no way
        in at all, since a new account has no passkey yet.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-3 space-y-3">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <input type="radio"
                 id="auth_emailed_sign_in_code"
                 name="emailed_sign_in"
                 value="code"
                 aria-describedby="auth_emailed_sign_in_code_hint"
                 <%= "checked" if @mechanism == :code %>
                 <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
                 class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 border-slate-500 text-purple-900">
          <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
            <label for="auth_emailed_sign_in_code" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
              A six-digit code they type
            </label>
            <p id="auth_emailed_sign_in_code_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
              Works when the mail is read somewhere else &mdash; a phone, a shared mailbox, a
              machine that is not the one they started on. This is also the only one of the two
              that can turn an unknown address into an account, because the account is made when
              the code is redeemed rather than when it is sent.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <input type="radio"
                 id="auth_emailed_sign_in_link"
                 name="emailed_sign_in"
                 value="link"
                 aria-describedby="auth_emailed_sign_in_link_hint"
                 <%= "checked" if @mechanism == :link %>
                 <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
                 class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 border-slate-500 text-purple-900">
          <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
            <label for="auth_emailed_sign_in_link" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
              A link they open
            </label>
            <p id="auth_emailed_sign_in_link_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
              One click, and it only works in the browser that asked for it &mdash; which is what
              stops anything that scans your mail from spending it, and what makes &ldquo;read it
              on my phone, finish on my laptop&rdquo; impossible. A link also cannot create an
              account: it has to hang on one that already exists, so with this chosen, new people
              arrive by invitation or through create-account.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset class="mt-6 border-t border-slate-200 pt-5 dark:border-slate-800">
      <legend class="sr-only">Optional ways in</legend>

      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <%#
          Hidden field first, then the box. A checkbox alone sends nothing when
          it is off, so "off" and "this client never rendered the control" would
          arrive as the same request. With the pair, the parameter is always
          there and always 0 or 1, and the controller refuses anything else.
        %>
        <input type="hidden" name="signup_with_code" value="0" autocomplete="off">
        <input type="checkbox"
               id="auth_signup_with_code"
               name="signup_with_code"
               value="1"
               aria-describedby="auth_signup_with_code_hint"
               <%= "checked" if @signup_with_code %>
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 rounded border-slate-500 text-purple-900">
        <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
          <label for="auth_signup_with_code" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
            An emailed code for an unknown address creates an account
          </label>
          <p id="auth_signup_with_code_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
            The shortest honest way to sign up: the code proves control of the mailbox, which is
            the only thing the account ever had to prove, and no password is invented along the
            way. Turn it off for an invite-only product, where an address arriving at the sign-in
            page should not become an account.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <%#
      Passwords are not one of the choices above, and this block is why they do
      not look like one. They are the weakest way in --
      the only credential that can be phished, guessed at scale, or arrive
      already known from somebody else's breach -- and a checkbox in a list of
      equals would say the opposite of that.

      Muted rather than amber, and the change of key matters. Amber on this
      page is a summons, and it is spent on exactly three things: a save that
      cannot happen, an unset Relying Party ID, and a Relying Party ID being
      changed out from under enrolled passkeys. Each wants doing now.

      Passwords want the opposite -- nothing is wrong, the default is already
      right, and the recommendation is to leave it alone. Amber here would be a
      fourth summons to something that isn't one, and a page where everything
      shouts is a page where nothing does.

      The signing keys further down carry these same colours, for the same
      reason. Between them they are the two blocks whose honest advice is "do
      not touch this", so they look alike and neither looks urgent.

      In dark mode the card sits at the page's own lightness, so it recedes
      into the page instead of standing off it like every other card, and the
      dark red rule is the only thing that draws it. Measured rather than
      eyeballed: the WCAG contrast RATIO is the wrong instrument for two
      adjacent dark surfaces -- it scores the console's existing, plainly
      visible cards at 1.01:1 -- so the surfaces were compared by OKLCH
      lightness, where the border stands 0.27 clear of its background.

      The TEXT is not muted, and that is deliberate. slate-400 on this ground
      is 3.04:1, under the 4.5:1 body-text floor, so the de-emphasis has to
      come from the surface. Washing out the words instead would make the one
      security decision on this page the hardest thing on it to read.
    %>
    <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-red-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:border-red-900 dark:bg-gray-950">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <input type="hidden" name="passwords_enabled" value="0" autocomplete="off">
        <input type="checkbox"
               id="auth_passwords_enabled"
               name="passwords_enabled"
               value="1"
               aria-describedby="auth_passwords_enabled_hint"
               <%= "checked" if @passwords_enabled %>
               <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
               class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 rounded border-slate-500 text-purple-900">
        <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
          <label for="auth_passwords_enabled" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300">
            Also let people sign in with a password
          </label>
          <p id="auth_passwords_enabled_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
            Off by default, and complete without them: an account can be created, verified,
            given a passkey and used for years with no password ever existing.
            Turning them on adds the weakest sign-in method, and the only credential here
            that somebody else's breach can hand to an attacker already working. Neither of the
            sign-in methods above it can be reused that way, or phished, or guessed at scale. Sometimes
            this is the right trade. It is never the free one.
          </p>
          <%#
            The chips sit on this card, not on the white ones, so they cannot
            reuse the slate-100 used everywhere else: this card's light ground
            is slate-50 and the two would touch. slate-200/slate-800 keeps them
            a step off it in both schemes.
          %>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
            Two settings only matter once this is on, and neither is here: the minimum length,
            which defaults to twelve characters, and the check that refuses a password already
            known to have been breached. That check is a call into somebody else's service, so it
            is code rather than a value —
            <code class="rounded bg-slate-200 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">password_breached</code>
            and
            <code class="rounded bg-slate-200 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">password_minimum_length</code>
            live in
            <code class="rounded bg-slate-200 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">config/initializers/sparrow_auth.rb</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%#
    Social sign-in, which the README has advertised since before it could be
    configured anywhere but a file.

    Two things have to be true for a provider to work: keys, which are here, and
    a gem, which cannot be. OmniAuth strategies are ordinary gems in the
    application's Gemfile, and no control panel can add one. So the page says
    which are missing and gives the exact line, rather than offering a control
    that produces a callback route raising on its first visitor.

    Keys are stored whether or not the gem is there. Somebody who pastes their
    credentials before running bundle install should find them afterwards.
  %>
  <section class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Google and Apple</h2>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Optional, and off until both a gem and a pair of credentials are here. Somebody who signs
      in this way still gets an account of yours; the provider proves who they are and nothing
      else.
    </p>

    <% unless @omniauth_present %>
      <p class="mt-3 rounded-md border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-700 dark:bg-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
        None of this works yet: <code class="font-mono">rodauth-omniauth</code> is not in your
        bundle, and it carries the whole feature. Add it, and the strategy for each provider you
        want, then restart.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
      <% @social.each do |provider| %>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100"><%= provider[:name] %></h3>

          <% unless provider[:gem_present] %>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
              Needs a gem this application does not have. Add it to your Gemfile and restart:
            </p>
            <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded-md bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-xs text-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200"><code>gem "<%= provider[:gem] %>"</code></pre>
          <% end %>

          <div class="mt-3 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
            <div>
              <label for="auth_social_<%= provider[:key] %>_id" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
                Client ID
              </label>
              <input type="text"
                     id="auth_social_<%= provider[:key] %>_id"
                     name="social[<%= provider[:key] %>][client_id]"
                     value="<%= provider[:client_id] %>"
                     autocomplete="off"
                     spellcheck="false"
                     <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
                     class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-sm text-slate-900 disabled:bg-slate-200 disabled:text-slate-500 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100 dark:disabled:bg-slate-800">
            </div>

            <div>
              <label for="auth_social_<%= provider[:key] %>_secret" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
                Client secret<%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" if provider[:secret_set] %>
              </label>
              <%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_field",
                    id: "auth_social_#{provider[:key]}_secret",
                    name: "social[#{provider[:key]}][client_secret]",
                    hint: nil,
                    describedby: "auth_social_#{provider[:key]}_secret_hint",
                    disabled: !@writable %>
              <p id="auth_social_<%= provider[:key] %>_secret_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                <%= provider[:secret_set] ? "Already set. Leave blank to keep it." : "From your #{provider[:name]} developer console." %>
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%#
    Folded away, because the honest advice is "do not touch this" and a section
    a developer has to scroll past on every visit reads like a section they are
    supposed to fill in. Two empty boxes among a page of boxes that DO want
    values is an invitation, and the value most likely to be invented by hand is
    the one that must never be.

    <details> rather than a script: keyboard reachable, findable by the
    browser's own find-in-page, and open on a printed page. With scripting off
    it still opens. The same element carries the auth-mail explanation further
    up, so the page has one disclosure idiom rather than two.

    Deliberately NOT collapsed once a key is set. Somebody who has one has a
    reason to look at it, and hiding the field they came for would be the
    disclosure working against them -- see the `open` attribute below.
  %>
  <% keys_set = @otp_secret.is_a?(Hash) && @otp_secret[:set] %>

  <%#
    The passwords card's colours, and for the passwords card's reason.
    Whatever is set here, the answer is almost always "leave it alone" -- so it
    gets the surface that recedes into the page and a dark red rule to mark it,
    rather than the amber this console spends on things that want doing now.

    A folded section is already saying "not for you"; shouting it in amber as
    well made the one block nobody should touch the loudest thing on the page.
  %>
  <details class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-red-200 bg-slate-50 p-5 dark:border-red-900 dark:bg-gray-950"
           <%= "open" if keys_set %>>
    <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300">
      Advanced — signing keys
      <span class="ml-1 font-normal">(you almost certainly do not need these)</span>
    </summary>

    <div class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      <p class="font-medium">Leave both blank unless you know you need them</p>
      <p class="mt-1">
        There is nowhere to get these from — no dashboard, no provider, no account. They are
        random strings, and if you leave them empty your application makes its own: Rails derives
        each one from the master key you already have, stable across restarts and deploys and
        distinct from every other derived key, so a leak of one does not compromise the others.
        That is the right answer for almost every application.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Set one only if you need to rotate it on its own schedule, or share it with another
        service that has to verify the same codes or tokens. Generate it, never invent it:
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        <code class="rounded bg-slate-200 px-1.5 py-1 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">bin/rails secret</code>
        — prints a fresh random value. Paste it into the matching box below.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Whichever you set, every code and token already issued under the previous key stops
        verifying the moment you save. Do it deliberately.
      </p>

      <%#
        Rails' own documentation rather than ours, deliberately. This is Rails
        behaviour, not SparrowKit behaviour: the derivation, the storage and the
        consequences of rotating a secret are all theirs, and a developer being
        careful about a signing key should be reading the source of truth rather
        than our summary of it. Ours would also go stale against theirs.
      %>
      <p class="mt-3 font-medium">Rails' documentation on this</p>
      <ul class="mt-1 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>
          <a href="https://guides.rubyonrails.org/security.html#custom-credentials"
             class="font-medium underline hover:no-underline"
             target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Securing Rails Applications — Custom credentials</a>
          — where secrets belong, why they must be long and random, and what changing one costs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://api.rubyonrails.org/classes/ActiveSupport/KeyGenerator.html"
             class="font-medium underline hover:no-underline"
             target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ActiveSupport::KeyGenerator</a>
          — the PBKDF2 derivation that produces these keys for you when you leave them blank.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
      One-time codes and API tokens are stored as HMAC digests rather than as themselves, and
      these are the keys those digests are computed with.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
      <div>
        <label for="auth_otp_secret" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300">
          One-time code key
          <% if @otp_secret.is_a?(Hash) && @otp_secret[:set] %>
            <%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_set" %>
          <% end %>
        </label>
        <%= render "sparrow_ui/console/secret_field",
              id: "auth_otp_secret",
              name: "otp_secret",
              hint: (@otp_secret[:hint] if @otp_secret.is_a?(Hash) && @otp_secret[:set]),
              describedby: "auth_otp_secret_hint",
              disabled: !@writable %>
        <p id="auth_otp_secret_hint" class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <% if @otp_secret.is_a?(Hash) && @otp_secret[:set] %>
            Already set. Leave this blank to keep it; paste a new one to replace it.
          <% else %>
            Empty is the right answer for most applications — Rails derives this key for you.
            To set your own, paste the output of
            <code class="rounded bg-slate-200 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">bin/rails secret</code>.
            It is written once and never shown again.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="mt-8 flex items-center gap-4">
    <button type="submit"
            <%= "disabled" unless @writable %>
            class="rounded-md bg-purple-900 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white disabled:bg-slate-300 disabled:text-slate-600 dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950 dark:disabled:bg-slate-700 dark:disabled:text-slate-400">
      Save authentication settings
    </button>
    <a href="<%= sparrow_ui.root_path %>"
       class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-600 hover:text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:hover:text-slate-100">
      Back to SparrowKit
    </a>
  </div>
</form>

<%#
  The sign-in harness. Development only, like everything under /sparrowkit.

  It exists because SparrowKit now serves no sign-in page of its own -- the
  screens belong to the application -- and because there is no obvious way to
  test what a viewer sees as opposed to an admin without three browsers and a
  lot of patience.

  WHAT IT IS NOT is a back door. The button below asks for a real code, reads it
  out of the mailbox the way a person reads their email, and redeems it. So it
  works only for verified accounts, it respects the once-a-minute limit, and it
  leaves a session that shows up on the sessions screen and can be ended there,
  exactly like anybody else's. A control that wrote the session directly would
  be shorter and would prove nothing.
%>
<section class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
  <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Sign in as somebody</h2>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
    For trying your own pages as the people who will use them. Pick an account
    and this signs your browser in as them, through the same flow they would go
    through &mdash; a real code, really redeemed. Only here, and only in
    development.
  </p>

  <% if @signed_in_as %>
    <p class="mt-3 rounded-md bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">
      You are currently signed in as <strong><%= @signed_in_as.email %></strong>.
      Signing in as somebody else replaces that.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <% unless @screens_generated %>
    <p class="mt-3 rounded-md border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-amber-900 dark:border-amber-700 dark:bg-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
      Nothing is routed at <code class="font-mono"><%= SparrowAuth.config.sign_in_path %></code>,
      so your application has no sign-in page yet &mdash; SparrowKit does not ship one.
      Rodauth serves its own at <code class="font-mono">/auth/login</code>; a page carrying your
      own navigation and words is yours to write. This harness works either way.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <% if @accounts.empty? %>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-md bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:bg-slate-800 dark:text-slate-300">
      There are no verified accounts yet. Run
      <code class="font-mono">bin/rails sparrow_auth:seed</code> to make one to start from.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <form action="<%= sign_in_as_path %>" method="post" class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-end gap-3">
      <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>" autocomplete="off">

      <div class="grow">
        <label for="auth_harness_account" class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
          Sign in as
        </label>
        <select id="auth_harness_account"
                name="account_id"
                class="mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border border-slate-500 bg-slate-100 px-3 py-2 text-sm text-slate-900 dark:bg-slate-950 dark:text-slate-100">
          <% @accounts.each do |account| %>
            <option value="<%= account.id %>"><%= account.email %></option>
          <% end %>
        </select>
      </div>

      <button type="submit"
              class="rounded-md bg-purple-900 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-purple-800 dark:bg-purple-300 dark:text-purple-950 dark:hover:bg-purple-200">
        Sign in
      </button>
    </form>

    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
      Only verified accounts are listed, because only a verified address is sent
      a code. A code can be issued once a minute per address, so signing in as
      the same person twice in quick succession is refused &mdash; by the rule
      rather than by this page.
    </p>
  <% end %>

  <%#
    A link rather than a second copy of the ceremony. Rodauth still serves the
    passkey pages, they are the ones a real user meets, and a WebAuthn
    enrolment reimplemented here would be the wrong thing tested.
  %>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
    To try a passkey, sign in above and then visit
    <a class="font-mono underline hover:text-purple-900 dark:hover:text-purple-300"
       href="<%= SparrowAuth.config.path_prefix %>/webauthn-setup"><%= SparrowAuth.config.path_prefix %>/webauthn-setup</a>.
    Passkeys bind to <code class="font-mono"><%= @effective_rp_id %></code>, which is what the box above sets.
  </p>
</section>

<h2 class="mt-10 text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">Not configured here</h2>
<p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  Everything else sparrow_auth exposes is set once, in
  <code class="rounded bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs dark:bg-slate-800">config/initializers/sparrow_auth.rb</code>,
  where the install generator left it with a comment explaining each choice. A setting that
  could be answered in two places is a setting that will eventually disagree with itself.
</p>
<ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">Anything that is code rather than a value</span> — the hooks your
    application is told through when somebody first signs in, verifies an address, accepts an
    invitation or creates an organization, and any role it declares for itself. A console
    cannot hold a lambda.
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">Social sign-in</span> — needs gems in your Gemfile as well as
    values, and each provider's options are shaped by its own strategy. Naming providers here
    without installing the gems would produce a callback route that raises on the first visitor.
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">The passkey origin</span> — derived from the request, which is
    right unless something in front of your application terminates TLS and rewrites the Host
    header. Set it there on the day that becomes true.
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="font-medium">Expiries, and where Rodauth serves its routes</span> — the
    verification and invitation windows, and the path prefix, which has to agree with wherever
    the engine is mounted because Rodauth runs above the Rails router rather than through it.
  </li>
</ul>

<script>
  // Progressive enhancement and nothing else.
  //
  // The Relying Party ID is the one setting on this page that a later edit
  // cannot put right, so this says so while it is being typed rather than
  // after it is saved. The same warning is already rendered by the server -- in
  // the hint under the box, and in the panel this toggles -- so with scripting
  // off the page loses a moment of timing and no information.
  //
  // Vanilla, inline, no build step. This console is served only to this
  // machine, only in development, and a developer console that needed a
  // toolchain to compare two strings would be the wrong trade.
  (function () {
    var input = document.getElementById("auth_webauthn_rp_id");
    var warning = document.getElementById("auth_rp_id_change");
    var toggle = document.getElementById("auth_webauthn_rp_id_override");
    var field = document.getElementById("auth_rp_id_override_field");
    if (!input || !warning || !toggle || !field) return;

    // The product name's disclosure works the same way and costs nothing to
    // drive from here. It has no warning attached: renaming a product breaks
    // nothing, unlike moving the domain.
    var nameToggle = document.getElementById("auth_webauthn_rp_name_override");
    var nameField = document.getElementById("auth_rp_name_override_field");
    if (nameToggle && nameField) {
      var refreshName = function () { nameField.hidden = !nameToggle.checked; };
      nameToggle.addEventListener("change", refreshName);
      refreshName();
    }

    var stored = input.getAttribute("data-stored-rp-id") || "";
    var inherited = toggle.getAttribute("data-inherited-rp-id") || "";

    // What passkeys would bind to if this were saved now: the box when the
    // switch is on, the address from the front page when it is off. The server
    // works the same value out; this only keeps up while somebody types.
    function effective() {
      return toggle.checked ? input.value.trim() : inherited;
    }

    function refresh() {
      field.hidden = !toggle.checked;

      // Nothing stored yet means nothing enrolled against it, so there is
      // nobody for a change to cost.
      warning.hidden = stored === "" || effective() === stored;
    }

    input.addEventListener("input", refresh);
    toggle.addEventListener("change", refresh);
    refresh();
  })();
</script>
